WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.800
[Music starts] [Michael F. Chiang, MD]&nbsp;
I'm an ophthalmologist by background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.916
and I actually started my career&nbsp;
in 2001 at Columbia University

00:00:06.094 --> 00:00:10.550
[Audio Description - not captioned]

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:16.851
as an NLM postdoctoral fellow. And since&nbsp;
then, I've spent 20 years in academia as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.851 --> 00:00:21.288
clinician scientist working at the interface&nbsp;
of ophthalmology and biomedical informatics.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.648
In November 2020, I started a new job at NIH&nbsp;
as director of the National Eye Institute.

00:00:27.648 --> 00:00:33.060
And I'm really excited to be a investigator here&nbsp;
at the National Library of Medicine because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.448
you know in a way, it's what&nbsp;
I started my career doing.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.528
At the National Eye Institute, our&nbsp;
mission is to eliminate vision loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.528 --> 00:00:44.100
and improve quality of life through vision&nbsp;
research, and that's an application domain.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.212
And at the National Library of Medicine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:50.421
what we do is methodological research&nbsp;
in biomedical informatics and data science.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.421 --> 00:00:54.669
And I think it's really important to&nbsp;
connect the methodology with the application.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.222
One of the things that I do is I&nbsp;
develop artificial intelligence&nbsp;methods

00:01:02.222 --> 00:01:06.120
to diagnose a pediatric retinal&nbsp;
disease called retinopathy of prematurity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.267
It's one of the leading causes of&nbsp;
blindness in babies around the world.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:16.498
Stevie Wonder is someone who went blind from ROP
in the 1950s before there was a treatment for it.

00:01:16.498 --> 00:01:20.340
And we've developed an AI system that&nbsp;
received FDA breakthrough status.

00:01:23.265 --> 00:01:27.516
And one of the things that we've been&nbsp;
doing is trying to find ways to apply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.516 --> 00:01:30.777
AI to real world problems in eye care for kids.

00:01:34.503 --> 00:01:38.400
Babies who are premature, and therefore&nbsp;
at risk for retinopathy of prematurity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.900
come disproportionately from urban&nbsp;
and rural medically underserved areas.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.960
And there are huge challenges&nbsp;
in terms of delivering eye care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.216
and health care overall, to those babies.

00:01:50.397 --> 00:01:53.574
And so, one of the things that I did at&nbsp;
the very beginning of my career is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.574 --> 00:01:58.866
we work to develop and validate telemedicine&nbsp;
systems for retinopathy of prematurity diagnosis.

00:01:58.866 --> 00:02:02.160
And now, what we're starting to&nbsp;
do is to experiment with - can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.289
we implement AI, artificial intelligence, into those

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.793
telemedicine systems to be able&nbsp;
to improve the accuracy and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.793 --> 00:02:14.640
quality of the diagnosis that&nbsp;
we're able to make remotely.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.815
There's never been a time in history where&nbsp;
we've had so much access to science and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.815 --> 00:02:24.723
technology as being applied to healthcare. And&nbsp;
I think one of the challenges for the future is

00:02:24.723 --> 00:02:27.537
how to move things like biomedical informatics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.537 --> 00:02:31.185
artificial intelligence and data science&nbsp;
into the mainstream of healthcare.

00:02:31.226 --> 00:02:32.770
[Music continues]

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.134
[Music fades to silence]

00:02:36.521 --> 00:02:37.591
[Silence]

